WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.340
...

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:20.140
Abelart Productions presents

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:36.060
In most families,

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:39.620
Stories are passed down
from one generation to the next,

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:43.060
And in other cases,
they sweep them under the rug.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:48.580
My family is like any other
and like no other.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:53.620
I am Harriet,
like my mother and my great-grandmother.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.180
I grew up in Europe with my parents,

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.660
my five sisters and my brother.

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:12.620
We now live
in different parts of the world

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.940
and we have our own families.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:16.900
As our children grow,

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:19.020
I'm realizing
how little they know

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:22.340
about the one thing
we all have in common.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:25.220
We are all related
to a man:

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:26.860
Edward Jones.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:32.460
His blood runs through our veins

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:36.020
And yet, its story
has remained a secret.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:39.340
What I know about Edward…

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.060
Edward... Jones?

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.860
The truth is, I don't know.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:55.100
Music composed and orchestrated by Philippe Kelly

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:01.100
Animation and drawing
by Christian Volckman

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:21.300
A film written, produced, and directed by Harriet Marin Jones

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.860
All I know is that he's my great-grandfather

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:30.780
and that he was the richest Black man in the United States.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:35.460
I know their base
was here in Chicago.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.580
King of Kings

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:47.140
Chasing Edward Jones

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:57.980
As the United States Senate,
we investigated

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.900
the tremendous importance
of organized crime,

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:03.220
which is even bigger
than we thought,

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:06.740
both from the point of view
of the criminal activity itself...

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.300
In 1951,

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:11.940
The Senate held a series of televised public hearings.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:15.340
which they exposed as
the criminal underworld

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:17.900
penetrated the affairs
of the United States.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:22.100
Edward Jones had to testify
before the investigating committee.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:27.420
The FBI wanted answers,
and so did I.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:31.620
The first time I heard about Edward Jones was from Mom.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:34.100
He used to mention it.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.100
Not much, very little,
but that's how I learned about him.

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:41.580
Actually, my mother
just told us

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:43.540
that his father was amazing.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:46.620
I don't know much about Edward Jones.

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:52.260
During my childhood,
I had never heard of him.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:55.300
She found it difficult to talk about him,
she never talked about him.

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:58.260
And when he did,
it was only half-spoken.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:00.580
So it was a great mystery,
at least to me.

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:03.180
Why have we never heard of that?

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:07.900
I guess something must have upset Mom.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:22.540
My name is Harriet,
and I am Edward Jones's daughter.

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:29.260
My father, Edward Jones,
was a gentleman.

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:31.420
Simply a good person.

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:35.300
This is what my mother
chose to pass on to us

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:37.100
as we grew up,

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.260
But I needed to delve deeper.

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:42.500
to find out
who Edward Jones really was

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:45.220
and unravel the mystery
that surrounded him.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:53.780
Who was this man
who shook the government?

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:59.260
After learning the truth,
I went in search of answers...

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:04.340
How did this shadow shape our family?

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:11.140
It was time to delve into the past

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:12.740
and question the present.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:33.700
My grandfather was a reverend
of the Baptist Church.

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:37.420
His name, of course, was Edward,
Edward Perry Jones,

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:38.980
like my father.

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:41.580
He had a large Baptist church.

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:45.220
He was very well known,
at least in Mississippi.

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:47.220
They had four children,

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:51.700
My father, Edward,
My uncle George, my uncle Mack

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:54.820
and a baby
who died at 3 years old.

00:06:56.300 --> 00:06:58.740
They had a very good life
in Mississippi.

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:02.220
All the children played instruments.

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:04.180
They rode horses,

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:07.980
not like ordinary people,

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:10.220
but in a riding arena.

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:21.140
My grandfather held a very important position.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:22.820
in the Republican party.

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:29.660
They had the first car in town.

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:33.660
When I was twelve, my father

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:37.420
He drove to the station

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:39.540
and he picked up people.

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:43.740
That's how you earned some money.

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:13.220
A white family lived very close by,

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:15.740
and the young woman, a white girl,

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:19.180
He came to borrow sugar
or something like that.

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:25.340
As she left, my father
walked her to the door.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:29.020
Just as some members of the KKK
were passing by.

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:41.420
Then they threatened my grandfather
and told him: “Be careful.”

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:04.460
My grandfather
didn't wait for something to happen.

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:08.020
He took the whole family
and went to Chicago.

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:15.580
Reverend Jones
uprooted his family

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:19.100
and he turned to the more liberal states in the North.

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:23.260
During the Great Migration,

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:26.940
More than 6 million African Americans
fled the South

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:29.460
to escape poverty
and the Ku Klux Klan.

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:35.740
My grandfather swore
never to return to Mississippi.

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:43.220
The railroad arrived,
through Mississippi and Alabama,

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:44.700
directly to Chicago.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:51.340
Men like my father,

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:55.020
who had skills
and aspirations for the future...

00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:58.740
They had fled because of the terror
to which they were exposed

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:01.140
and they arrived in Chicago

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:04.580
to have better opportunities
and to be able to vote.

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:18.620
The great migration was initiated

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:21.100
by the newspaper
"The Chicago Defender"

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:24.660
since it was impossible to live in the South
at that time

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:26.060
and find a job.

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:27.980
What jobs could you find?

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:34.780
A black man who left the South could find two jobs in a day.

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:41.180
I could work in the warehouse
and in the steel mill.

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:50.300
My father created "The Defender"
in the 1930s/40s.

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:55.140
We sold a quarter of a million
newspapers per week.

00:10:56.300 --> 00:11:01.260
They all came together because they had nothing else but the Black community.

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:25.300
This is Evanston,
north of Chicago.

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:32.100
This is the first church
in Illinois

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:34.820
where Reverend Edward Perry Jones officiated,

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:37.220
father of the Jones brothers.

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:41.140
He was a Baptist pastor
of fire and blood.

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:45.060
So this is where the Jones brothers' journey begins.

00:11:54.820 --> 00:11:56.420
When my father was in Evanston,

00:11:56.580 --> 00:12:01.340
I was going to Northwestern University
and studying medicine.

00:12:02.900 --> 00:12:07.220
Edward Jones pursued studies
worthy of a pastor's son,

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:11.340
But in the North, discrimination
took other forms.

00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:14.500
When he entered Northwestern,

00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:17.540
an elite university
almost exclusively white,

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:21.220
He realized immediately
that they had always underestimated him

00:12:21.380 --> 00:12:23.020
because of the color of their skin.

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:27.500
Instead of worrying him,
it gave him wings.

00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:30.900
While I was studying,

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:33.580
He owned a car company.

00:12:36.420 --> 00:12:38.500
My grandfather earned a living

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:41.180
running
his own taxi business.

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:44.300
The city seemed to offer great opportunities.

00:12:44.460 --> 00:12:46.180
to ambitious young people.

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:51.780
But the American dream
was not within everyone's reach.

00:13:00.820 --> 00:13:03.900
My father and my uncle George told me about it:

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:07.300
When they walked on the sidewalk,
they had to get off,

00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:10.140
to make way for the whites.

00:13:13.180 --> 00:13:15.420
Sharing public space
with Black people

00:13:15.580 --> 00:13:17.420
They found it unbearable.

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:21.420
So they put into practice
a series of customs and practices,

00:13:21.580 --> 00:13:24.140
later recognized
as constitutional

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:26.260
by the Supreme Court
of the United States,

00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:30.380
for what could be called
American apartheid,

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:33.300
to keep
blacks not only separate,

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:36.380
but in a permanent state
of inferiority.

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:40.220
Lower quality schools,
lower quality theaters,

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:42.740
polling stations
very, very far away,

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:47.940
constant intimidation
and forbidden to raise your head.

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:10.700
The One Drop Rule
is a racist law,

00:14:10.860 --> 00:14:16.300
which says that anyone
in the United States

00:14:16.460 --> 00:14:20.460
with a drop of black blood,
that's the one-drop rule,

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:23.380
It is considered
to be completely black.

00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:26.060
If you and I had lived in Mom's time,

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:30.340
We wouldn't have access
to white restaurants, fountains…

00:14:31.940 --> 00:14:34.380
Depending on what color you are,

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:37.460
What will happen to you
will be dramatically different.

00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:39.900
Listening to them,

00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:43.660
I realized that neither my children,
nor my nephews,

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:46.860
nor anyone who wasn't 100% white,

00:14:47.020 --> 00:14:48.620
would have been able to integrate

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:52.420
and we would all have become
second-class citizens.

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:58.340
The words "white supremacy"
began to echo in my head…

00:14:59.940 --> 00:15:02.580
I couldn't even imagine it before.

00:15:02.740 --> 00:15:07.220
It wasn't easy.
It's still not easy today.

00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:11.780
Yesterday there were disturbances, not riots,
more like peaceful protests

00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:14.580
for the murder
of a Black teenager.

00:15:15.580 --> 00:15:16.460
So...

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:22.460
It is noticeably more difficult
for someone of a different skin color

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:28.340
to achieve as much success
as he did back then.

00:15:40.940 --> 00:15:43.700
At that time,
everything was divided according to ethnicity,

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:45.660
an Irish neighborhood,
a German neighborhood,

00:15:45.820 --> 00:15:48.660
an Italian neighborhood,
a Polish neighborhood.

00:15:48.820 --> 00:15:51.820
That's roughly 90%
of the Anglo-Saxon part of Chicago.

00:15:53.020 --> 00:15:54.460
There were African American neighborhoods.

00:15:54.620 --> 00:15:57.620
Most white people
didn't go to those neighborhoods,

00:15:57.780 --> 00:16:01.500
And I don't think many black people went to white neighborhoods either.

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:08.500
In August 1919,

00:16:08.660 --> 00:16:15.180
A young black man approached the beach
on 31st Street by Lake Michigan.

00:16:18.900 --> 00:16:22.420
Black people could only swim
in one part of that lake.

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:27.020
And if they swam outside of that area...

00:16:27.940 --> 00:16:31.900
They were violating the rule.

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:37.180
There were gangs that joined forces
against the young man and drowned him.

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:51.180
The population that had fled the South

00:16:51.340 --> 00:16:54.580
He began to defend himself;
there were race riots.

00:16:56.220 --> 00:17:02.060
And the young men who had served
during the First World War

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:06.380
They defended themselves,
because they were experienced soldiers.

00:17:13.460 --> 00:17:17.500
It was the first time
in the history of this country

00:17:17.660 --> 00:17:24.820
that there were reprisals
for attacks against Black people...

00:17:25.580 --> 00:17:27.220
in this country.

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:10.140
The Chicago race riots of 1919
The Chicago race riots of 1919

00:18:10.300 --> 00:18:13.820
They left more than a thousand African Americans homeless.

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:16.380
when the white people
burned their houses.

00:18:20.660 --> 00:18:23.780
After fleeing the South,
they were forced to flee again.

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:24.860
One more time.

00:18:28.540 --> 00:18:32.380
The Red Summer is considered
the awakening of Black America.

00:18:38.540 --> 00:18:40.980
My grandfather was 22 years old

00:18:41.140 --> 00:18:43.540
And these riots
marked him for life.

00:18:46.700 --> 00:18:48.220
Without thinking twice,

00:18:48.380 --> 00:18:51.220
He left Northwestern
to enroll at Howard,

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:54.900
a university exclusively for Black people in Washington DC.

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:06.260
When I was studying medicine,

00:19:06.420 --> 00:19:09.700
He worked on a train
to support himself.

00:19:09.860 --> 00:19:12.820
He was the supervisor
of the restaurant car.

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:16.540
He was said to organize dice and card games.

00:19:16.700 --> 00:19:20.300
in a rear carriage with some of the regular passengers.

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:25.500
Back then, it was probably
easier for a Black person

00:19:25.660 --> 00:19:27.220
to dedicate oneself to the game

00:19:27.380 --> 00:19:31.620
To think I would end up
practicing medicine in a hospital.

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:39.580
These high-level professions
that are currently held by African Americans

00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:42.460
were not within reach
of many African Americans.

00:19:42.620 --> 00:19:45.460
To succeed in business,
he had to take

00:19:45.620 --> 00:19:48.380
a side road
that led him to great success.

00:19:53.740 --> 00:19:58.580
In 1924, my great-grandfather,
Reverend Edward Perry Jones,

00:19:58.740 --> 00:20:01.740
He died of a heart attack
at the age of 52.

00:20:03.700 --> 00:20:06.860
Convinced that all men are created equal,

00:20:07.020 --> 00:20:09.060
His sermons
have profoundly impacted

00:20:09.220 --> 00:20:11.420
to his family and his congregation.

00:20:12.820 --> 00:20:14.380
But since he had already left,

00:20:14.540 --> 00:20:18.340
His three children were free
to explore their own path.

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:20.260
After the death of the reverend

00:20:20.420 --> 00:20:22.420
and the return
of the older children home,

00:20:22.580 --> 00:20:26.460
They end up getting involved in the Policy game
and the numbers scam.

00:20:26.620 --> 00:20:32.300
The Policy is what is known today as the state lottery.

00:20:32.940 --> 00:20:35.540
But long before
the state lottery,

00:20:35.700 --> 00:20:39.740
There was an illegal game
called Policy,

00:20:39.900 --> 00:20:43.420
owned, operated, and controlled
by black men

00:20:43.580 --> 00:20:46.860
called Policy Kings
or Number Kings.

00:20:53.100 --> 00:20:55.620
The Policy scheme
was a form of play

00:20:55.780 --> 00:20:58.340
affordable to the general public.

00:20:58.500 --> 00:21:00.460
It was what it gave
to men or women

00:21:00.620 --> 00:21:02.860
currents the opportunity
to get rich.

00:21:03.020 --> 00:21:05.860
Ordinary people
played this lottery.

00:21:06.020 --> 00:21:11.260
There were many places where they could
bet on the South Side of Chicago,

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:15.580
which is where this
began and flourished

00:21:15.740 --> 00:21:18.940
in the African American community
known as Bronzeville.

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:42.540
African Americans in Chicago,
especially in the South,

00:21:42.700 --> 00:21:47.780
They had a rich tradition of casino games, dice games, and card games.

00:21:47.940 --> 00:21:49.900
even before the arrival of the Policy.

00:21:50.060 --> 00:21:52.340
When the Policy
was in its early days,

00:21:52.500 --> 00:21:54.860
organized crime,
within the Black community,

00:21:55.020 --> 00:21:58.540
It was already flourishing
in the form of illegal gambling.

00:22:00.740 --> 00:22:06.020
Uncle Mack worked
in a Policy office.

00:22:06.180 --> 00:22:08.060
He told my father:

00:22:08.220 --> 00:22:13.820
"Well, this isn't bad,"
and my father said, "Okay."

00:22:14.100 --> 00:22:16.980
My father understood numbers.

00:22:17.140 --> 00:22:20.820
He would take a long list of numbers

00:22:20.980 --> 00:22:25.940
and was able
to solve them immediately.

00:22:26.100 --> 00:22:27.940
You bet 5 cents on a number...

00:22:28.900 --> 00:22:31.140
And if it works, you can win $5.

00:22:31.300 --> 00:22:34.100
But many people can bet
$10 on a number,

00:22:34.260 --> 00:22:38.100
And if that number comes up,
they win $500 or $1,000.

00:22:39.180 --> 00:22:40.620
When my grandfather died,

00:22:40.780 --> 00:22:47.700
I think my grandmother had insurance for
$16,000 or something like that

00:22:47.860 --> 00:22:50.580
and gave it to his children.

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:00.700
You need to get to State Street.

00:23:01.380 --> 00:23:04.180
Because the station
was right next to Wabash.

00:23:09.060 --> 00:23:09.980
We are looking for...

00:23:10.140 --> 00:23:11.380
The corner of Michigan
and 48th Street.

00:23:11.540 --> 00:23:12.460
48, okay.

00:23:22.820 --> 00:23:25.740
This is the first Policy Station

00:23:25.900 --> 00:23:27.980
owned by the Jones brothers.

00:23:28.940 --> 00:23:32.740
People came and placed their bets.

00:23:37.460 --> 00:23:39.620
The front half
was a tailor shop.

00:23:39.780 --> 00:23:44.580
If you enter for the first time,
you'll see clothes, you'll see fabric.

00:23:45.820 --> 00:23:47.140
Probably some kind

00:23:47.300 --> 00:23:50.060
with a measuring tape
around the neck,

00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:51.580
That's just the facade.

00:23:51.740 --> 00:23:53.500
It has to look legitimate.

00:23:54.940 --> 00:23:58.300
But in the back
there's Policy in action.

00:24:01.420 --> 00:24:05.220
A bunch of guys sitting around,
counting all this money,

00:24:05.380 --> 00:24:07.180
getting ready to go to the bank.

00:24:09.460 --> 00:24:14.100
There is a great rivalry
between this Policy station

00:24:14.260 --> 00:24:17.660
and the Policy Station
of the barbershop across the street.

00:24:17.820 --> 00:24:19.740
Mack used to work for that guy.

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:24.780
Ezra Leake was one of the most well-known guys

00:24:24.940 --> 00:24:27.020
and one of the leaders of the time.

00:24:30.100 --> 00:24:31.420
To the Jones brothers,

00:24:31.580 --> 00:24:34.500
People liked them,
people respected them.

00:24:34.940 --> 00:24:37.620
Ezra Leake
was just a street hustler.

00:24:39.260 --> 00:24:42.620
The rivalry was intense.

00:24:45.980 --> 00:24:47.740
My grandmother told them:

00:24:47.900 --> 00:24:52.340
"Whatever you do with Policy,
always be completely honest."

00:24:53.100 --> 00:24:56.420
"Whoever earns money, whoever they are,
you give it to them immediately."

00:24:56.580 --> 00:24:58.580
And people appreciated it.

00:25:02.540 --> 00:25:03.740
In less than two years,

00:25:03.900 --> 00:25:08.580
They became the number one policy publication in the city.

00:25:09.220 --> 00:25:11.700
How many Policy operators

00:25:11.860 --> 00:25:13.820
They were relegated
to the background

00:25:13.980 --> 00:25:16.020
Because of the Jones brothers?

00:25:16.180 --> 00:25:19.500
They had to be very well-liked
and enjoyed a great deal of trust.

00:25:28.220 --> 00:25:31.220
Since color was a barrier,
my grandfather turned to a color

00:25:31.380 --> 00:25:34.460
who was supposed to not suffer
from any discrimination:

00:25:34.620 --> 00:25:36.900
Green, the color of money.

00:25:37.060 --> 00:25:38.860
It would be their key to freedom.

00:25:42.700 --> 00:25:46.100
This is one of the most historic buildings in Chicago.

00:25:47.340 --> 00:25:51.220
And your grandfather and his brothers
had a lot to do with this.

00:25:51.380 --> 00:25:52.780
Okay, let's go.

00:25:56.380 --> 00:25:59.780
When they were ready
to draw the numbers at noon,

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:01.980
Many people
found their way this way.

00:26:02.140 --> 00:26:03.540
They surrounded the alley,

00:26:03.700 --> 00:26:07.860
They passed through a back door
to the drawing rooms.

00:26:12.020 --> 00:26:15.580
Sometimes, my father would take my brother and me.

00:26:15.740 --> 00:26:18.260
to the Policy station

00:26:18.420 --> 00:26:20.580
to see how they drew the numbers.

00:26:21.140 --> 00:26:27.420
I saw Ed watching
the numbers get mixed up.

00:26:32.460 --> 00:26:37.780
The winning numbers were obtained by
drawing numbers from a drum.

00:26:37.940 --> 00:26:43.420
The roulette wheel was spun by turning the crank of the drum.

00:26:43.580 --> 00:26:48.100
where the numbers were mixed up
and the winning number came out.

00:26:54.020 --> 00:26:55.660
It was like a meeting place

00:26:55.820 --> 00:26:58.700
where they stopped
and talked and gossiped

00:26:58.860 --> 00:27:02.020
about what was happening
while the numbers were playing.

00:27:02.180 --> 00:27:06.420
So they went to the Policy Station
to have fun

00:27:06.580 --> 00:27:08.220
and try to earn some money

00:27:08.380 --> 00:27:11.540
with the five, ten and twenty-five cent coins.

00:27:15.100 --> 00:27:17.540
The stock market crash changed everything.

00:27:18.220 --> 00:27:22.580
On October 29, 1929,
known as Black Tuesday,

00:27:22.740 --> 00:27:24.860
The Great Depression began.

00:27:30.700 --> 00:27:32.900
We used to eat rats for dinner.

00:27:34.420 --> 00:27:38.260
My mother used to send my brother and me to look for rats in the river.

00:27:38.420 --> 00:27:41.100
You see, and she fried them
with oil lamps.

00:27:42.100 --> 00:27:45.340
Almost everyone played Policy.

00:27:46.060 --> 00:27:50.620
Policy was their only hope
of being able to have dinner.

00:27:55.420 --> 00:27:59.540
Mystical on a certain level, it's that idea
that everyone is searching,

00:27:59.700 --> 00:28:00.980
that changes his life

00:28:01.140 --> 00:28:03.780
and the circumstances
in which he finds himself.

00:28:03.940 --> 00:28:06.100
Sitting
on a small, cold-water floor

00:28:06.260 --> 00:28:08.940
on the south side of Chicago,
all you had to do

00:28:09.100 --> 00:28:11.620
It was just a matter of getting your number
and everything would be fine.

00:28:12.740 --> 00:28:16.100
Winning or losing didn't matter. Only the possibility of winning mattered.

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:19.180
And knowing that if you won,
you would get paid.

00:28:21.900 --> 00:28:24.740
What began
as a penny lottery

00:28:24.900 --> 00:28:31.540
It became
a multi-million dollar business.

00:28:31.700 --> 00:28:34.100
And it became
the biggest wheel in Policy,

00:28:34.260 --> 00:28:37.820
not only from Chicago, but from the country.

00:28:43.700 --> 00:28:44.820
In those days,

00:28:44.980 --> 00:28:49.140
They generated the equivalent of fifteen or twenty thousand dollars a day

00:28:49.300 --> 00:28:51.620
only with small change.

00:28:52.420 --> 00:28:58.060
In 1929, 2% of the population
earned more than $10,000 a year.

00:28:58.220 --> 00:29:03.620
My uncle John Woelly had a Policy wheel with your grandfather Ed Jones.

00:29:03.780 --> 00:29:07.900
The Vienna bathhouse
was located at 41st and Indiana.

00:29:08.060 --> 00:29:13.140
And in the 1930s,
they didn't treat Black people.

00:29:13.300 --> 00:29:18.420
He went there; he was a big guy,
with very fair skin,

00:29:18.580 --> 00:29:20.740
And they told him
that they didn't serve black people.

00:29:20.900 --> 00:29:25.420
He returned the next day and bought it.
He paid in cash and dismissed everyone.

00:29:32.540 --> 00:29:35.420
Black people
were playing Policy more than ever.

00:29:35.580 --> 00:29:37.780
When the whites
started playing too,

00:29:37.940 --> 00:29:42.660
The Policy Kings were on a roll,
and my grandfather was earning more than anyone.

00:29:44.660 --> 00:29:47.180
Since he was rich and successful,

00:29:47.340 --> 00:29:51.380
Doors began to open,
even in New York.

00:29:54.340 --> 00:29:57.420
Black people were not allowed
to enter the Cotton Club,

00:29:57.580 --> 00:29:59.940
But my father was very well-known
at that time,

00:30:00.100 --> 00:30:01.540
So they let him in.

00:30:12.100 --> 00:30:16.340
My mother used to dance at the Cotton Club.

00:30:16.500 --> 00:30:19.020
I think I was part of the choir.

00:30:51.260 --> 00:30:54.940
He always talked about how beautiful she was.

00:30:57.340 --> 00:30:59.820
He invited her to the table.

00:30:59.980 --> 00:31:01.620
And they fell in love.

00:31:12.180 --> 00:31:14.220
He was extremely charming.

00:31:14.380 --> 00:31:15.940
He was a classy man,

00:31:16.100 --> 00:31:20.700
very intelligent, very calm,

00:31:20.860 --> 00:31:22.460
with a great sense of humor.

00:31:25.140 --> 00:31:27.460
Lena Horne was also around.

00:31:28.020 --> 00:31:30.900
He loved to say jokingly:

00:31:31.060 --> 00:31:34.220
"Well, I had the option
of choosing Lena Horne,

00:31:34.420 --> 00:31:36.860
"And then I had the option of Lydia."

00:31:37.020 --> 00:31:40.180
And he said, "I think
I made the best choice!"

00:31:48.820 --> 00:31:51.540
He left the next day,

00:31:51.700 --> 00:31:55.700
But they continued
sending letters and talking to each other.

00:31:58.820 --> 00:32:04.060
My mother told me
that one day they went out partying.

00:32:04.220 --> 00:32:07.700
He got my father a little drunk

00:32:07.860 --> 00:32:09.340
and they got married.

00:32:33.860 --> 00:32:35.420
When we lived in Chicago,

00:32:35.580 --> 00:32:38.460
It was in a building,
and each one had a floor.

00:32:38.620 --> 00:32:40.780
My father was on the first floor.

00:32:40.940 --> 00:32:42.660
Uncle Mack
was on the second floor,

00:32:42.820 --> 00:32:44.780
Uncle George in the third one.

00:32:45.220 --> 00:32:47.500
My grandmother
was on the other side of the street.

00:32:47.660 --> 00:32:53.140
When my grandfather died, a doctor
wanted to marry my grandmother,

00:32:53.300 --> 00:32:56.420
because she was still a young,
attractive woman,

00:32:56.580 --> 00:33:01.140
And her three sons told her,
"No, you can't get married."

00:33:01.300 --> 00:33:03.580
So she told them,
"Okay, if I don't get married,

00:33:03.740 --> 00:33:06.260
"You have to come and visit me
every day,

00:33:06.420 --> 00:33:09.260
"Because I don't want to be alone."

00:33:09.420 --> 00:33:12.540
So her children would visit her.

00:33:13.660 --> 00:33:16.420
She was a very strict woman.

00:33:19.220 --> 00:33:22.460
I don't think my grandmother liked my mother very much.

00:33:22.620 --> 00:33:25.740
Maybe because she was a chorus girl,
I really don't know,

00:33:25.900 --> 00:33:27.420
But I don't think he'd like it.

00:33:27.580 --> 00:33:32.060
I remember my mother telling me
that when I was 25 years old

00:33:32.220 --> 00:33:33.460
he started smoking

00:33:33.620 --> 00:33:38.420
because my grandmother
said something disrespectful to her.

00:33:40.820 --> 00:33:44.780
My father never sided with my mother against her mother.

00:33:44.940 --> 00:33:48.220
to say,
"Okay, leave her alone," or whatever.

00:33:48.380 --> 00:33:50.900
Because she was very fragile.

00:33:57.380 --> 00:34:00.020
My parents had a house
on Michigan Avenue,

00:34:00.180 --> 00:34:03.580
It was a block from the school, so
that's where I went to school

00:34:03.740 --> 00:34:06.940
and where the rich black children went.

00:34:08.820 --> 00:34:12.100
I had no idea
that Harriet was going there too.

00:34:15.260 --> 00:34:17.260
We lived a privileged life.

00:34:17.420 --> 00:34:18.900
When I say that,

00:34:19.060 --> 00:34:22.020
I mean, we had people
who looked after us,

00:34:22.180 --> 00:34:24.460
We had a cook, we had a chauffeur.

00:34:28.820 --> 00:34:31.980
My father was a carpenter
for all the Jones brothers.

00:34:32.140 --> 00:34:33.260
He built his own houses.

00:34:34.500 --> 00:34:40.660
I met your grandfather at a birthday party at his house.

00:34:40.820 --> 00:34:45.340
It was Harriet's birthday,
and she asked me to cut her hair.

00:34:45.500 --> 00:34:47.500
She was five years old, I was seven.

00:34:49.700 --> 00:34:53.980
I made the mistake of cutting it
and I cut all his hair off.

00:34:54.140 --> 00:34:57.500
And my father kicked me in the ass.

00:34:57.660 --> 00:35:00.580
He said to me, "Do you know who his father is?"

00:35:00.740 --> 00:35:03.260
I was seven years old,
I didn't understand what was happening.

00:35:13.900 --> 00:35:16.260
This place is known as the place

00:35:16.420 --> 00:35:20.180
where all the famous people
come to have fun and hang out.

00:35:56.820 --> 00:35:59.420
These guys were making
tons of money,

00:35:59.580 --> 00:36:03.860
partying with the rich
and the politicians and all that.

00:36:04.260 --> 00:36:10.220
Some guys are going to come after them
to keep them away from the money.

00:36:13.540 --> 00:36:15.340
In the 1930s,

00:36:15.500 --> 00:36:19.100
This block was a fortress.

00:36:19.900 --> 00:36:22.780
Bodyguards all over the neighborhood.

00:36:24.700 --> 00:36:27.660
In Chicago, we had bodyguards.
I remember that.

00:36:28.660 --> 00:36:31.300
Have you ever heard
that your father was violent?

00:36:31.460 --> 00:36:33.900
My father? He was never violent.

00:36:34.060 --> 00:36:37.340
Never. Never. Never.

00:36:40.620 --> 00:36:43.380
- Was he carrying a weapon?
- Of course, he never carried a weapon.

00:36:43.540 --> 00:36:45.260
Everyone was carrying a weapon.

00:36:45.980 --> 00:36:49.900
He did too, to protect himself.
Because they were trying to kill him.

00:36:50.060 --> 00:36:52.580
She wants to make him a saint.

00:36:52.740 --> 00:36:55.140
But, honey, he's a gangster.

00:36:55.300 --> 00:36:56.740
My father was never a gangster.

00:36:56.900 --> 00:36:58.420
A gangster is a gangster.

00:36:58.580 --> 00:37:01.540
A gangster will do anything
to get his way.

00:37:01.700 --> 00:37:03.020
to survive.

00:37:03.180 --> 00:37:04.740
That's normal.

00:37:07.860 --> 00:37:12.380
Al Capone had his own alliance
with the Kings of Politics.

00:37:15.900 --> 00:37:18.780
Before Al Capone
left the city

00:37:18.940 --> 00:37:22.660
and was imprisoned
for tax evasion in 1931,

00:37:22.820 --> 00:37:29.700
Much of the illegal liquor
that came south from the north

00:37:29.860 --> 00:37:32.500
It was transported
in coffins of dead black people

00:37:32.660 --> 00:37:34.860
who were returning home
to be buried.

00:37:36.380 --> 00:37:39.300
Since they helped him
to transport liquor,

00:37:39.460 --> 00:37:42.260
He left them alone
and their betting business.

00:37:43.660 --> 00:37:47.500
In Chicago, the organized crime group is called the Mob.

00:37:47.820 --> 00:37:49.620
Also called the Outfit.

00:37:52.420 --> 00:37:54.220
Al Capone was a gangster.

00:37:54.380 --> 00:37:56.620
Ed Jones was a gambler.

00:37:56.780 --> 00:38:00.140
Al Capone used guns
to get what he wanted,

00:38:00.300 --> 00:38:03.180
Ed Jones to protect what he had.

00:38:04.540 --> 00:38:07.500
They didn't go out into the street
to shoot people.

00:38:07.660 --> 00:38:09.620
They had firearms
for protection

00:38:09.780 --> 00:38:14.540
of the assailants who might
try to take some of the money.

00:38:16.220 --> 00:38:19.580
One thing about the Policy
is that it was just an illegal lottery.

00:38:19.740 --> 00:38:22.860
At the Outfit,
there was gambling, prostitution.

00:38:23.020 --> 00:38:25.260
During Prohibition,
there was alcohol.

00:38:27.700 --> 00:38:31.340
The Kings of Policy
didn't impose their lottery on anyone,

00:38:31.500 --> 00:38:33.260
They did not invade other communities,

00:38:33.420 --> 00:38:36.180
They basically minded their own business.

00:38:36.340 --> 00:38:37.620
unlike the Mob.

00:38:37.780 --> 00:38:42.180
Violence was a prerogative
of the Mob, that's what they were.

00:38:42.340 --> 00:38:45.140
They used violence
to promote their activities.

00:38:51.060 --> 00:38:54.180
The Mob was involved
in the kidnapping of Mack Jones

00:38:54.340 --> 00:38:56.780
in order to be able to enter by force.

00:38:59.820 --> 00:39:03.220
If there were problems,
they sent Ted Roe.

00:39:03.380 --> 00:39:06.100
He was like his front man.

00:39:08.380 --> 00:39:13.100
Teddy Roe
paid a kind of ransom,

00:39:13.260 --> 00:39:15.780
but they didn't harm him
during the kidnapping.

00:39:15.940 --> 00:39:17.940
They didn't beat him, they didn't torture him.

00:39:21.740 --> 00:39:26.180
1931 was an orgy of murders
in the underworld.

00:39:28.380 --> 00:39:32.580
David Jiles, a close friend of Ed Jones,

00:39:32.740 --> 00:39:35.220
He was murdered by gangsters.

00:39:38.140 --> 00:39:41.100
When he was assassinated,
that changed everything.

00:39:41.860 --> 00:39:43.900
His murder provoked

00:39:44.060 --> 00:39:48.180
the largest gathering of Policy Kings
in the city's history.

00:39:49.780 --> 00:39:52.900
And that's when Ed
started talking about organization.

00:39:53.420 --> 00:39:57.780
We can fight individually,
but they take us out one by one.

00:39:57.940 --> 00:40:02.140
Or we can unite and deal with this as a unit.

00:40:02.300 --> 00:40:05.100
Ed made that happen.

00:40:05.620 --> 00:40:10.020
This is the beginning of the rise
of the Policy Union.

00:40:10.180 --> 00:40:14.460
Ed Jones was the founder and leader
of the union.

00:40:14.620 --> 00:40:17.300
So we're talking about
a union of 12 people.

00:40:17.460 --> 00:40:19.940
Three of these people
are the Jones brothers,

00:40:20.100 --> 00:40:23.140
and the rest
are a collection of men

00:40:23.300 --> 00:40:27.100
that operate independently
their own Policy businesses.

00:40:27.900 --> 00:40:29.340
As union leader,

00:40:29.500 --> 00:40:33.060
My grandfather was nicknamed "The Great Ed" by some,

00:40:33.220 --> 00:40:35.100
"Emperor Jones" by others,

00:40:35.260 --> 00:40:37.740
But for most,
he was the "King of Kings".

00:40:43.860 --> 00:40:46.020
The era of prohibition
is over.

00:40:46.180 --> 00:40:48.780
Everyone is looking for
new sources of income.

00:40:49.580 --> 00:40:53.540
It was during the Depression, and it turns out that
those who earned the most money

00:40:53.700 --> 00:40:56.540
These were the black guys
from the South Side of Chicago.

00:40:59.060 --> 00:41:02.260
In those days,
it wasn't easy to do what he did.

00:41:02.780 --> 00:41:06.820
These guys used to show up
wearing their ties.

00:41:06.980 --> 00:41:10.900
They took care of themselves,
of their physical appearance, with respect.

00:41:11.060 --> 00:41:15.260
I loved that.
It really impressed me.

00:41:19.420 --> 00:41:23.220
When you're young, you see that, and you think,
"That's what I'm going to do."

00:41:23.380 --> 00:41:25.580
You want to be what you see.

00:41:26.140 --> 00:41:27.860
It was a sign of hope.

00:41:28.780 --> 00:41:31.500
He had all the intelligence,

00:41:31.660 --> 00:41:36.420
eloquence, sensitivity.

00:41:36.580 --> 00:41:38.860
He had it all. And the passion.
He had it all.

00:41:39.020 --> 00:41:41.060
That's what it takes
to be a leader.

00:41:41.220 --> 00:41:43.220
And he was definitely a leader.

00:41:43.380 --> 00:41:46.340
And at that moment, it was difficult.

00:41:48.060 --> 00:41:49.900
The money allowed my grandfather

00:41:50.060 --> 00:41:52.140
to lead a respectable life on the outside,

00:41:52.300 --> 00:41:55.100
But behind the facade,
everything was illegal.

00:41:55.260 --> 00:41:57.180
because the Policy was illegal.

00:41:57.340 --> 00:42:00.700
His influence, however,
in Chicago was considerable

00:42:00.860 --> 00:42:03.700
and it reached as far as the Town Hall.

00:42:05.220 --> 00:42:08.420
The common feeling at the time
was that the Republicans

00:42:08.580 --> 00:42:12.060
They couldn't control the city
because of gangsterism

00:42:12.220 --> 00:42:14.340
and to all the murders

00:42:14.500 --> 00:42:17.380
by the mafia against Capone
and his rivals.

00:42:21.900 --> 00:42:25.820
The outgoing Republican mayor
was Thompson.

00:42:25.980 --> 00:42:27.260
Big Bill Thompson.

00:42:29.780 --> 00:42:32.380
They're the ones who want you to believe

00:42:32.540 --> 00:42:37.700
that Chicago is the crime center
of our country,

00:42:37.860 --> 00:42:39.500
it isn't true.

00:42:42.220 --> 00:42:47.060
All the money generated
from illegal gambling

00:42:47.220 --> 00:42:49.460
and illegal liquor,

00:42:49.620 --> 00:42:51.860
He wants to control it for himself.

00:42:52.620 --> 00:42:56.180
Send police,
police raids...

00:42:56.340 --> 00:42:58.460
They repress everyone.

00:42:59.820 --> 00:43:03.860
Anton Cermak, Democrat, enters.

00:43:05.100 --> 00:43:08.700
Cermak's problem
is that he doesn't like black people.

00:43:10.180 --> 00:43:13.740
But the Democrats
are looking for positions.

00:43:14.220 --> 00:43:16.940
They knew they couldn't do it alone.

00:43:17.100 --> 00:43:21.900
So they're looking for the most influential Black man in Chicago.

00:43:22.060 --> 00:43:24.780
At that time,
he was not a preacher,

00:43:24.940 --> 00:43:28.780
neither a businessman
nor a banker,

00:43:28.940 --> 00:43:31.340
It was Ed Jones.

00:43:35.260 --> 00:43:39.180
Historically, African Americans
were Republicans in Chicago.

00:43:39.340 --> 00:43:40.100
Ed Jones had

00:43:40.260 --> 00:43:43.380
a key role in the shift
from Republican to Democrat.

00:43:43.940 --> 00:43:47.700
To be able to bring together
the entire South Side of Chicago

00:43:47.860 --> 00:43:51.300
behind their candidate
was of vital importance.

00:43:51.460 --> 00:43:52.660
There were many mayors,

00:43:52.820 --> 00:43:56.140
Big Bill Thompson,
who was a very corrupt person,

00:43:56.300 --> 00:44:01.460
Anton Cermak followed, attempting to
rectify part of the situation,

00:44:01.620 --> 00:44:05.700
but he also wanted
control of the people at Policy,

00:44:05.860 --> 00:44:07.740
particularly because of their votes.

00:44:09.580 --> 00:44:11.180
Cermak told us:

00:44:11.340 --> 00:44:15.340
"If your people switch their allegiance
to the Democratic Party,

00:44:15.500 --> 00:44:17.820
"The police actions will cease."

00:44:20.060 --> 00:44:23.500
"Vote the way we tell you to vote,
and we'll leave you alone."

00:44:27.580 --> 00:44:31.500
In reality, it wasn't about
ideological loyalty to a party.

00:44:31.660 --> 00:44:36.100
It was about which party was going to provide you with the most protection.

00:44:36.260 --> 00:44:40.500
so that your criminal enterprise
could operate smoothly,

00:44:40.660 --> 00:44:44.020
with the protection
of the police and other politicians,

00:44:44.180 --> 00:44:47.340
making sure your business
was not interrupted.

00:44:48.300 --> 00:44:50.460
All these men,
even the Italian Mob,

00:44:50.620 --> 00:44:54.100
They are a product of their time,
of social disorganization,

00:44:54.260 --> 00:44:56.980
of poverty, of a government
that doesn't do its job.

00:44:57.140 --> 00:45:00.620
There is no organized crime
without a corrupt government.

00:45:19.140 --> 00:45:21.340
The police also came and placed bets.

00:45:21.500 --> 00:45:23.860
Bets were accepted
from all over the world.

00:45:24.020 --> 00:45:25.620
I placed everyone's bets.

00:45:26.700 --> 00:45:28.140
When there were raids,

00:45:28.300 --> 00:45:32.700
We all knew because they would call me
and say: "Get ready, they're coming."

00:45:33.700 --> 00:45:35.420
"Prepare your things, prepare one,

00:45:35.580 --> 00:45:37.900
"They're going to need to
take someone with them."

00:45:42.500 --> 00:45:45.380
There comes a time
when money and everything else

00:45:45.540 --> 00:45:47.220
It ends here.

00:45:49.580 --> 00:45:54.660
The police who worked for them
would stop here at the agreed-upon time

00:45:54.820 --> 00:45:58.540
And the guys came out with bags of money and put them in the car.

00:45:58.700 --> 00:46:03.180
Sometimes he was your great-uncle Mack,
sometimes he was your great-uncle George.

00:46:04.540 --> 00:46:06.860
Ed rarely did it himself.

00:46:09.340 --> 00:46:11.420
But they loaded the cars with Ted Roe.

00:46:11.580 --> 00:46:15.540
And they had police officers
escorting them to the bank

00:46:15.700 --> 00:46:17.140
to deposit this money.

00:46:17.300 --> 00:46:19.260
It's an all-cash business.

00:46:24.900 --> 00:46:27.500
They had a contract
with the Hawthorne Milk Company

00:46:27.660 --> 00:46:32.260
to distribute milk to all
Black public schools.

00:46:32.420 --> 00:46:34.580
All the trucks
that came in and out of here

00:46:34.740 --> 00:46:38.420
to deliver milk to schools
they also collected for Policy.

00:46:41.700 --> 00:46:44.820
They had a company that was doing very well,

00:46:44.980 --> 00:46:47.900
earned
more than a million dollars a year

00:46:48.060 --> 00:46:50.980
and employed 10,000 people.

00:46:52.620 --> 00:46:54.180
The Jones brothers,

00:46:54.340 --> 00:46:56.940
thanks to their education,

00:46:57.100 --> 00:46:58.860
They were more than just players.

00:46:59.020 --> 00:47:02.820
They were lawyers, accountants.
They were businessmen.

00:47:06.500 --> 00:47:09.820
The Jones brothers funded
medical and law students.

00:47:09.980 --> 00:47:15.500
They helped them set up
doctors' and dentists' offices.

00:47:15.660 --> 00:47:21.140
I should call them the Robin Hoods
of Chicago during the Depression

00:47:21.300 --> 00:47:23.660
because they took care of people.

00:47:29.540 --> 00:47:32.580
Ed Jones was famous
for giving away food at Christmas.

00:47:32.740 --> 00:47:35.700
He also invested in Joe Louis,
the boxer.

00:47:35.860 --> 00:47:38.820
He brought a lot of pride
to the Black community.

00:47:46.380 --> 00:47:47.780
He invested in his people.

00:47:47.940 --> 00:47:50.020
He had large warehouses in the south,

00:47:50.180 --> 00:47:52.620
in areas
that large companies didn't touch.

00:47:53.860 --> 00:47:55.580
It empowered the people who lived there

00:47:55.740 --> 00:47:57.740
by giving
the same business options

00:47:57.900 --> 00:48:00.140
that the people
from the northern part of the city.

00:48:06.580 --> 00:48:09.860
Time magazine
declared Bronzeville

00:48:10.020 --> 00:48:13.540
such as the Black Business Center
of the United States.

00:48:15.660 --> 00:48:19.060
Policy's activities
provided income for people.

00:48:19.220 --> 00:48:21.220
The millions of dollars
that were generated

00:48:21.380 --> 00:48:25.500
were reinvested in the community
in the production of legal businesses

00:48:25.660 --> 00:48:30.820
So it was a thriving underground economy.

00:48:32.660 --> 00:48:36.540
My grandfather must have known that,
by empowering the Black community,

00:48:36.700 --> 00:48:38.940
He would become a target.

00:48:40.220 --> 00:48:41.860
However, he did it.

00:48:42.020 --> 00:48:45.980
opening doors
and setting an example for others.

00:48:48.180 --> 00:48:51.060
But the challenge remained enormous.

00:48:51.220 --> 00:48:55.460
Being alone on the South Side of Chicago,
which was the Black Belt,

00:48:55.620 --> 00:48:57.820
It quickly became
a problem.

00:48:57.980 --> 00:49:01.380
Population density
in Bronzeville

00:49:01.540 --> 00:49:05.500
It was four times higher
than that of neighboring white communities,

00:49:05.660 --> 00:49:07.820
like Hyde Park, Kenwood.

00:49:08.420 --> 00:49:09.580
In Hyde Park, Kenwood,

00:49:09.740 --> 00:49:15.100
The population density was 8,100 inhabitants per square kilometer.

00:49:15.260 --> 00:49:19.380
In Bronzeville, it was 32,400 per square kilometer.

00:49:20.300 --> 00:49:24.140
So we were constantly moving
to have more space.

00:49:25.940 --> 00:49:30.140
Because the neighborhoods were overcrowded, the schools were too.

00:49:30.300 --> 00:49:32.300
So when the parents
found

00:49:32.460 --> 00:49:36.220
a place where their children could
go to a less overcrowded school,

00:49:36.380 --> 00:49:38.060
They were moving so that their children could...

00:49:38.220 --> 00:49:40.740
could receive
a better education.

00:49:47.220 --> 00:49:49.580
My father took us
to a very good school

00:49:49.740 --> 00:49:53.140
and asked that we be admitted
to the school and they told him no,

00:49:53.300 --> 00:49:56.460
"We don't accept black children,
we don't accept children of color."

00:50:02.940 --> 00:50:06.740
During the era of slavery
and segregation, whites

00:50:06.900 --> 00:50:09.500
They always made sure
that the blacks

00:50:09.660 --> 00:50:13.740
They should have at least a minimum of education,
because education is power.

00:50:13.900 --> 00:50:17.900
And that led to
an active political effort

00:50:18.060 --> 00:50:20.620
to deny people of color
a decent education

00:50:20.780 --> 00:50:23.700
by guaranteeing whites

00:50:23.860 --> 00:50:27.220
access to the best teachers, schools, and buildings.

00:50:27.380 --> 00:50:31.220
The integration of schools
provoked the strongest reaction

00:50:31.380 --> 00:50:34.220
in the targets of all civil rights actions.

00:50:34.380 --> 00:50:36.860
And very quickly,
we saw white families

00:50:37.020 --> 00:50:41.380
pulling their children out of schools
if there were Black children enrolled,

00:50:41.540 --> 00:50:44.980
to enroll them in private schools, private academies,

00:50:45.140 --> 00:50:46.900
That was also seen in Chicago.

00:50:51.740 --> 00:50:55.620
I think that's why
we moved to France.

00:50:59.180 --> 00:51:01.140
Although they had very fair skin

00:51:01.300 --> 00:51:03.660
and sometimes they could pass for white,

00:51:03.820 --> 00:51:06.860
The institutions
knew they were Black.

00:51:08.860 --> 00:51:10.580
My grandfather searched, once again,

00:51:10.740 --> 00:51:13.180
ways to break
the invisible chains of the past.

00:51:15.500 --> 00:51:18.060
Just as his father had done
14 years earlier,

00:51:18.220 --> 00:51:21.980
She moved so that her children
could grow up in a country

00:51:22.140 --> 00:51:24.020
where color wasn't an issue.

00:51:45.180 --> 00:51:47.100
My father bought an apartment

00:51:47.260 --> 00:51:49.460
at number 1
Pershing Boulevard.

00:51:52.220 --> 00:51:55.100
I used to go to school there.

00:51:57.500 --> 00:51:59.900
They were very happy
to be in France

00:52:00.060 --> 00:52:01.900
because there was no discrimination.

00:52:02.060 --> 00:52:04.260
They could go wherever they wanted,

00:52:04.420 --> 00:52:07.820
They could do whatever they wanted,
and they loved it.

00:52:09.020 --> 00:52:11.180
They learned a little French.

00:52:14.180 --> 00:52:16.780
My parents met
Josephine Baker,

00:52:16.940 --> 00:52:21.100
I remember her very well
with her cheetah.

00:52:26.780 --> 00:52:28.980
My parents enjoyed life.

00:52:29.140 --> 00:52:33.700
I remember my mother all dressed up,
she looked gorgeous.

00:52:36.540 --> 00:52:39.580
My mother had a very difficult childhood.

00:52:40.260 --> 00:52:43.380
He grew up in Hell's Kitchen
in New York.

00:52:48.660 --> 00:52:50.620
I didn't have enough to eat.

00:52:51.300 --> 00:52:54.740
His mother wasn't the best kind of mother, let's put it that way.

00:52:55.140 --> 00:52:57.700
When I was little, around 4 years old,

00:52:57.860 --> 00:53:01.220
Someone was giving him a little alcohol to drink.

00:53:01.380 --> 00:53:03.100
So she danced.

00:53:05.620 --> 00:53:08.700
She was raped when she was 13 years old.

00:53:10.300 --> 00:53:13.820
She had a daughter, my sister Dorothy.

00:53:16.580 --> 00:53:18.580
A very nice family took her in

00:53:18.740 --> 00:53:20.820
and went to live
in West Virginia.

00:53:23.860 --> 00:53:27.180
My mother
started working very young.

00:53:27.780 --> 00:53:31.140
One of the jobs I had
was as an elevator operator

00:53:32.700 --> 00:53:36.380
And then, suddenly,
he realized

00:53:36.540 --> 00:53:39.140
that she was a young and beautiful woman.

00:53:39.300 --> 00:53:41.260
So he traveled to Europe

00:53:41.420 --> 00:53:42.700
and her life changed.

00:53:48.860 --> 00:53:50.820
His beginnings were very difficult,

00:53:50.980 --> 00:53:53.860
And I think that's when
my grandmother started dancing

00:53:54.020 --> 00:53:57.020
to try to survive.

00:54:08.380 --> 00:54:12.980
In Paris, she was at the Revue Nègre
with Josephine Baker.

00:54:13.140 --> 00:54:14.780
They were roommates.

00:54:50.020 --> 00:54:52.460
His happiness in Paris was short-lived.

00:54:52.620 --> 00:54:54.860
Once again, they had to flee...

00:54:56.220 --> 00:54:59.100
We were living there
when the war started.

00:54:59.260 --> 00:55:01.420
And I remember the sirens,

00:55:01.580 --> 00:55:05.300
We had to run to the closet
and put on a gas mask.

00:55:08.420 --> 00:55:12.060
I can still smell the gas mask.

00:55:14.860 --> 00:55:17.300
My father bought us
the last tickets

00:55:17.460 --> 00:55:19.860
on a ship
returning to the United States.

00:55:29.940 --> 00:55:33.180
A family friend
told us that the Germans

00:55:33.340 --> 00:55:37.100
Their headquarters were located
in the apartment where we lived.

00:55:53.700 --> 00:55:56.500
Edward Jones and his family
returned to Chicago,

00:55:56.660 --> 00:55:59.900
where his empire
was then in danger.

00:56:00.060 --> 00:56:03.060
During the two years
abroad, they changed,

00:56:03.220 --> 00:56:04.820
But not the United States.

00:56:04.980 --> 00:56:08.060
The Kings of Policy
tried in Chicago

00:56:08.220 --> 00:56:13.900
28 months for tax evasion
for the King of Policy

00:56:14.060 --> 00:56:16.460
I know my great-grandfather
was in jail.

00:56:16.620 --> 00:56:19.740
Why did he go to jail?
I didn't even know.

00:56:19.900 --> 00:56:22.980
I have no idea why.

00:56:23.140 --> 00:56:24.380
Why did he go to jail?

00:56:24.540 --> 00:56:27.980
When the feds
pursued the Jones brothers,

00:56:28.140 --> 00:56:31.100
Actually,
it had nothing to do with the game

00:56:31.260 --> 00:56:35.020
neither by hiding money
nor by filing tax returns,

00:56:35.180 --> 00:56:37.140
That's why they got caught.

00:56:37.300 --> 00:56:38.980
Actually, that wasn't the point.

00:56:39.140 --> 00:56:41.540
It was
the Black community in general

00:56:41.700 --> 00:56:45.100
and of the most influential people
who made a difference.

00:56:45.260 --> 00:56:47.220
They had to be marginalized.

00:56:47.380 --> 00:56:49.980
so that the dark agenda
could never progress

00:56:50.140 --> 00:56:53.420
to the point of being at the same level as that of white people.

00:56:53.580 --> 00:56:54.860
That couldn't be.

00:56:56.980 --> 00:57:01.300
All the siblings and the mother
were named for tax evasion.

00:57:01.460 --> 00:57:05.860
The deal he made was to get his mother out of trouble first.

00:57:06.020 --> 00:57:09.140
She never had to answer for any of that legally.

00:57:09.300 --> 00:57:14.500
Ed made sure his mother
never spent a single day in jail.

00:57:18.420 --> 00:57:22.100
Ed offered a check,
which was not accepted.

00:57:24.020 --> 00:57:27.660
The Jones brothers felt
that if they did not plead guilty,

00:57:27.820 --> 00:57:30.460
The three of them would go to jail.

00:57:30.620 --> 00:57:35.940
Ed agreed to be the one
who would take responsibility.

00:57:42.060 --> 00:57:46.700
He assumed his brother George
wouldn't survive prison.

00:57:46.860 --> 00:57:50.860
I wouldn't have the mental capacity
to endure it.

00:57:51.020 --> 00:57:53.020
So he bore the sentence.

00:57:54.620 --> 00:57:56.940
After accusing them,

00:57:57.100 --> 00:58:00.100
after freezing
their bank accounts,

00:58:00.260 --> 00:58:04.300
They ended up returning $850,000 to them.

00:58:04.460 --> 00:58:08.620
That's admitting without acknowledging it
that the government was wrong.

00:58:10.380 --> 00:58:13.060
The white ruling class
that controlled all of this

00:58:13.220 --> 00:58:15.180
He never wanted to talk about it.

00:58:15.340 --> 00:58:18.860
They never wanted to acknowledge
that a man of color

00:58:19.020 --> 00:58:21.220
could have more power,
more knowledge

00:58:21.380 --> 00:58:23.380
and more insightful than they.

00:58:31.620 --> 00:58:35.300
I was just a kid
and I didn't know I was in jail.

00:58:35.460 --> 00:58:38.140
They told me
he had gone to war.

00:58:50.100 --> 00:58:54.060
In prison, they gave him an IQ test.

00:58:54.220 --> 00:58:57.300
They had never seen an IQ test like that.

00:58:57.460 --> 00:59:00.060
So they put him
in solitary confinement.

00:59:19.060 --> 00:59:22.860
Sam Giancana had run
an illegal whiskey distillery.

00:59:23.020 --> 00:59:27.020
They became friends, probably
because they were both from Chicago.

00:59:29.460 --> 00:59:33.540
Sam Giancana
was a born criminal

00:59:33.700 --> 00:59:38.660
and became famous for his vicious
killer personality.

00:59:38.820 --> 00:59:43.340
He dedicated his entire life
to criminal activities.

00:59:43.500 --> 00:59:46.740
African Americans
were not held in high esteem.

00:59:46.900 --> 00:59:51.740
I think he presented himself
to Sam Giancana as an equal.

00:59:53.260 --> 00:59:56.180
Sam Giancana offered my father a job working for him.

00:59:56.340 --> 00:59:59.260
and that he would give her
about a hundred dollars a week.

00:59:59.420 --> 01:00:01.860
And my father said,
"Are you crazy?"

01:00:02.220 --> 01:00:06.860
It was then that he revealed

01:00:07.020 --> 01:00:09.940
to this white Italian gangster

01:00:10.100 --> 01:00:14.380
How much money was there
in the Policy business.

01:00:18.140 --> 01:00:20.220
He was definitely bragging,
everyone was.

01:00:20.380 --> 01:00:22.060
That's what people do in prison.

01:00:22.220 --> 01:00:24.260
It's what men do outside.

01:00:24.420 --> 01:00:25.940
They boast about what they have.

01:00:26.100 --> 01:00:27.700
Huge mistake.

01:00:27.860 --> 01:00:30.500
Of course, I shouldn't have said anything.

01:00:30.660 --> 01:00:32.620
But it's easy to say that
after the fact.

01:00:32.780 --> 01:00:35.500
Perhaps he wanted to impress others.

01:00:35.660 --> 01:00:39.580
Maybe he wanted to protect himself, maybe... I don't know.

01:00:39.740 --> 01:00:42.140
Of course,
I shouldn't have spoken.

01:00:43.180 --> 01:00:44.660
His two years in prison

01:00:44.820 --> 01:00:47.980
They marked a turning point
in my grandfather's life

01:00:48.140 --> 01:00:52.060
because Sam
Giancana's ambitions knew no bounds.

01:00:53.220 --> 01:00:54.700
When Jones left,

01:00:54.860 --> 01:00:56.940
wanted to get involved with the Mob,

01:00:57.100 --> 01:00:59.780
who set up jukeboxes
all over the South Side of Chicago

01:00:59.940 --> 01:01:02.420
because the Outfit controlled them.

01:01:02.580 --> 01:01:05.500
He saw it as a way
to truly expand his empire.

01:01:05.660 --> 01:01:07.340
And that was a big mistake.

01:01:08.580 --> 01:01:10.060
The Outfit's way of working

01:01:10.220 --> 01:01:13.460
It's that once they get you in,
they own you.

01:01:13.620 --> 01:01:16.700
Once they got him into the jukebox business,

01:01:16.860 --> 01:01:18.140
They wanted more.

01:01:20.580 --> 01:01:22.380
Mr. Jones and his colleagues

01:01:22.540 --> 01:01:26.220
They did not look favorably upon
this interaction with the Italians.

01:01:26.380 --> 01:01:29.980
Theo Reo didn't like Italians,
and he was right.

01:01:32.860 --> 01:01:36.300
The Giancana family, like Al Capone, don't play.

01:01:36.900 --> 01:01:38.380
You get eliminated.

01:01:38.820 --> 01:01:40.860
Chicago is a tough city, honey.

01:01:45.460 --> 01:01:47.020
Your business is growing.

01:01:47.180 --> 01:01:49.860
Then they tell you:
"Now you have to pass it on to us."

01:01:50.020 --> 01:01:51.860
"Now you have a new partner."

01:01:52.860 --> 01:01:55.780
And when you resist,
they give you a warning.

01:01:55.940 --> 01:01:58.860
The warning was the hospital.

01:01:59.020 --> 01:02:01.940
They would break your legs
with a baseball bat.

01:02:02.100 --> 01:02:03.820
You would go out and come back to work.

01:02:05.260 --> 01:02:08.140
At the second warning,
they buried you.

01:02:08.540 --> 01:02:10.860
So you didn't have another chance.

01:02:11.020 --> 01:02:12.580
That's how they operated.

01:02:18.740 --> 01:02:25.300
King of Policy dies in accident

01:02:26.900 --> 01:02:30.140
I remember my father,
he picked up the phone

01:02:30.300 --> 01:02:34.020
And I knew intuitively
that something terrible had happened.

01:02:37.180 --> 01:02:39.740
My husband told me
that he had been murdered.

01:02:39.900 --> 01:02:43.100
He was shot by gangsters.

01:02:43.260 --> 01:02:47.580
I don't know which gangsters,
they never confirmed it to me.

01:02:47.740 --> 01:02:52.100
Lydia didn't want to talk about it.
And Ed never mentioned it.

01:02:52.260 --> 01:02:54.860
All we knew
was that he had been murdered.

01:02:55.020 --> 01:02:57.780
That was kept very, very secret.

01:03:01.300 --> 01:03:03.460
The Italians began
to threaten Ed

01:03:03.620 --> 01:03:05.340
with kidnapping their children.

01:03:05.500 --> 01:03:08.460
Ed had many bodyguards
around the school,

01:03:08.620 --> 01:03:12.860
He said to himself, "Okay, that's enough. My family is in danger."

01:03:13.020 --> 01:03:16.300
That's when he took his family and went to Mexico.

01:03:17.820 --> 01:03:19.740
He fled with his brother,

01:03:19.900 --> 01:03:24.740
allegedly taking
$14 million to Mexico.

01:03:25.100 --> 01:03:30.780
$14 million equivalent
to about $224 million today

01:03:31.260 --> 01:03:34.020
I give your grandfather credit for washing his hands of it then.

01:03:34.180 --> 01:03:38.900
There are many who earn a lot,
but it never seems enough to them.

01:03:40.900 --> 01:03:43.620
We always traveled by train,

01:03:43.780 --> 01:03:47.460
because my father
had a phobia of flying.

01:03:51.900 --> 01:03:55.900
Once again, my grandfather and his family
had to flee.

01:03:56.060 --> 01:03:57.740
Neither money nor power

01:03:57.900 --> 01:04:01.300
were enough to keep them safe
in the United States.

01:04:03.380 --> 01:04:04.780
It was time to move,

01:04:04.940 --> 01:04:06.180
one more time.

01:04:07.820 --> 01:04:11.860
We were privileged; we had
our own compartments.

01:04:12.220 --> 01:04:13.580
When we arrived in Texas,

01:04:13.740 --> 01:04:16.140
My father told us:
"Lower the blinds."

01:04:16.300 --> 01:04:19.380
"We didn't want people
to come in and kick us out."

01:04:19.540 --> 01:04:22.100
Because there was a lot of discrimination.

01:04:50.660 --> 01:04:52.260
When we arrived in Mexico,

01:04:52.420 --> 01:04:57.340
It was just my father,
my mother, and the three of us.

01:04:58.860 --> 01:05:02.620
We realized we weren't going back when we started school.

01:05:04.980 --> 01:05:09.860
I think the teacher didn't like having someone who didn't speak Spanish.

01:05:10.020 --> 01:05:11.980
He put me at the back of the classroom,

01:05:12.140 --> 01:05:18.340
He gave me a book and said,
"Write the whole book."

01:05:18.500 --> 01:05:23.340
So believe me,
I can write very well in Spanish.

01:05:26.700 --> 01:05:32.220
My father imported cars
from the United States to Mexico.

01:05:36.020 --> 01:05:39.340
What my father liked most about Mexico

01:05:39.500 --> 01:05:41.860
It was his factory
of hand-painted fabrics

01:05:42.020 --> 01:05:45.700
And everyone who worked there
loved it.

01:05:45.860 --> 01:05:48.060
It was very successful.

01:05:48.820 --> 01:05:52.340
When Ed went to Mexico,
everything was legal.

01:05:52.500 --> 01:05:56.180
Lydia had a wonderful shop
on Reforma Boulevard.

01:05:56.340 --> 01:05:59.180
She had models, she had beautiful clothes.

01:06:03.260 --> 01:06:06.140
While all this is happening,
Ted takes charge.

01:06:06.300 --> 01:06:09.620
So every time Ed is away,

01:06:09.780 --> 01:06:14.220
Ted runs the business
because he's the one they trust the most.

01:06:17.300 --> 01:06:20.420
We went to the house in Mexico.
He told me, "This is Quincy Jones."

01:06:20.580 --> 01:06:25.060
He said, "Are you the little bastard
who cut my daughter's hair?"

01:06:25.220 --> 01:06:27.460
I said, "Oh my God, no!"

01:06:27.900 --> 01:06:30.340
Josephine Baker came to Mexico.

01:06:31.420 --> 01:06:34.180
My father financed
his tour of the United States.

01:06:35.020 --> 01:06:37.460
She was a very brave woman.

01:06:37.620 --> 01:06:39.620
Travelling by train was amazing.

01:06:39.780 --> 01:06:43.180
because my father
wouldn't let her fly either.

01:06:43.980 --> 01:06:47.620
She approached those who were sitting down and said to them:

01:06:47.780 --> 01:06:50.060
"What's happening in the United States is terrible."

01:06:50.220 --> 01:06:52.820
"How can you be discriminated against like this?"

01:06:53.420 --> 01:06:57.140
My mother would tell her, "Josephine,
you're going to get us all killed."

01:07:00.540 --> 01:07:02.500
The debut was in Boston.

01:07:02.660 --> 01:07:04.940
Very few people came.

01:07:05.100 --> 01:07:06.900
The place was empty.

01:07:07.060 --> 01:07:10.460
I remember turning around
and it was very empty.

01:07:13.700 --> 01:07:16.380
People admired her greatly
in France.

01:07:16.540 --> 01:07:17.900
I was used to that.

01:07:18.060 --> 01:07:20.220
He was awarded the Legion of Honor

01:07:20.380 --> 01:07:22.900
and he was a prominent figure in France.

01:07:23.740 --> 01:07:26.860
And in the United States,
she was just another Black woman.

01:07:27.020 --> 01:07:30.580
Josephine Baker, back from a
long tour of the United States,

01:07:30.740 --> 01:07:33.060
She is welcomed
by her Parisian friends.

01:07:43.940 --> 01:07:46.380
While the Jones brothers
were in Mexico,

01:07:46.540 --> 01:07:50.620
Ted Roe ran the day-to-day business in the "Windy City".

01:07:50.780 --> 01:07:53.420
But my grandfather,
as head of the company,

01:07:53.580 --> 01:07:56.140
He often made discreet
trips to Chicago

01:07:56.300 --> 01:07:58.580
to attend to their various matters.

01:08:00.500 --> 01:08:02.900
They were coming from the Ben Franklin store

01:08:04.500 --> 01:08:08.660
And when they were parking,

01:08:08.820 --> 01:08:11.740
A car appeared out of nowhere.

01:08:14.700 --> 01:08:18.460
A guy jumped out of the car and put
a shotgun to Brock's face.

01:08:18.620 --> 01:08:23.060
Brock was the Jones brothers' longtime driver and bodyguard.

01:08:23.220 --> 01:08:24.900
He took Ed out of the car,

01:08:25.060 --> 01:08:29.500
He threw it into the back seat
of the getaway car, and drove off.

01:08:31.820 --> 01:08:34.660
Ed's wife, Lydia,
began screaming for help.

01:08:35.740 --> 01:08:39.540
At that moment,
a police car arrived.

01:08:39.700 --> 01:08:43.500
She told them what had happened.
The chase began.

01:08:55.100 --> 01:08:57.820
Really? Had he been kidnapped?

01:08:58.700 --> 01:09:01.860
I'd never heard of that.
Was it in Mexico?

01:09:02.020 --> 01:09:05.380
My grandmother used to tell me the story
of how he was kidnapped.

01:09:05.540 --> 01:09:06.700
They took him out of the car

01:09:06.860 --> 01:09:09.660
And she told me
that she ran after the car

01:09:09.820 --> 01:09:12.100
and they started shooting at him.

01:09:15.060 --> 01:09:19.460
Ed Jones was kidnapped
by the Mob to force their way in.

01:09:19.620 --> 01:09:22.020
Jones refused
to let the Mob in,

01:09:22.180 --> 01:09:24.660
So now they were going after him.

01:09:25.820 --> 01:09:28.260
When my father was kidnapped,
I didn't know.

01:09:28.420 --> 01:09:32.260
People didn't talk to their children about those kinds of things.

01:09:36.500 --> 01:09:40.380
He told me that he had his eyes constantly bandaged,

01:09:40.540 --> 01:09:43.620
They put a gun to his head

01:09:43.780 --> 01:09:46.940
and thought
that perhaps it was his last day.

01:09:50.220 --> 01:09:53.180
The original ransom
was $250,000.

01:09:53.340 --> 01:09:58.740
But they announced that the demand
had dropped to $100,000.

01:09:58.900 --> 01:10:03.780
The offer was made and Ed was released
in the middle of the night.

01:10:03.940 --> 01:10:06.340
The kidnappers
were never arrested.

01:10:06.500 --> 01:10:09.660
Freedom for a kidnapped black man

01:10:09.820 --> 01:10:12.340
I imagine Sam Giancana
told your grandfather:

01:10:12.500 --> 01:10:14.300
"You have lost your privileges."

01:10:14.460 --> 01:10:17.060
"You can go out and take
the money you've earned,

01:10:17.220 --> 01:10:19.820
"But from now on,
it's my business."

01:10:19.980 --> 01:10:23.140
The FBI is looking for Jones

01:10:28.460 --> 01:10:31.540
It is said that Sam Giancana went
to see Teddy Roe at his bar

01:10:31.700 --> 01:10:34.340
and Teddy Roe punched him
and knocked him to the ground,

01:10:34.500 --> 01:10:37.740
which proves that Teddy Roe
was a fearless man

01:10:37.900 --> 01:10:42.140
capable of striking the boss
of organized crime.

01:10:43.980 --> 01:10:47.540
Ted carried two pistols;
he was a very good shot

01:10:47.700 --> 01:10:49.900
and he didn't accept bullshit from anyone.

01:10:50.060 --> 01:10:52.940
Sam Giancana
liked to put pressure on people.

01:10:53.100 --> 01:10:54.980
Ted wouldn't be bossed around.

01:11:01.700 --> 01:11:05.180
Sam Giancana tried to assassinate him.

01:11:07.180 --> 01:11:11.940
But Teddy Roe and his bodyguard
killed a member of the Outfit,

01:11:12.100 --> 01:11:15.260
Marcio Carfalo's brother,
Fat Lenny.

01:11:18.940 --> 01:11:22.420
Policy King shoots man
trying to kidnap him

01:11:24.980 --> 01:11:28.660
He wasn't like other people
when the gangsters showed up.

01:11:28.820 --> 01:11:31.580
Most people were giving up.
Teddy Roe didn't.

01:11:31.740 --> 01:11:33.060
He fought against them.

01:11:33.220 --> 01:11:37.060
It was something completely unheard of
at that particular time.

01:11:49.420 --> 01:11:52.300
In 1951, my grandfather was summoned

01:11:52.460 --> 01:11:55.860
to appear
before the Kefauver committee.

01:11:56.940 --> 01:11:59.540
Once again, the rope was tightened.

01:11:59.700 --> 01:12:04.740
Let's look at the interstate connection
of the big business of Policy

01:12:04.900 --> 01:12:06.740
and other types of games.

01:12:06.900 --> 01:12:10.020
And our research
will also show

01:12:10.180 --> 01:12:13.660
some of the economic effects
of organized crime.

01:12:13.820 --> 01:12:14.860
The Kefauver Commission

01:12:15.020 --> 01:12:18.740
It was one of the first government entities to expose

01:12:18.900 --> 01:12:23.660
the amount of money being
earned through crime.

01:12:23.820 --> 01:12:28.700
He revealed something that 90% of Americans didn't know.

01:12:28.860 --> 01:12:33.420
Have you contributed to any political organization?

01:12:33.580 --> 01:12:35.660
during your stay in Chicago?

01:12:36.500 --> 01:12:40.620
Well, I wouldn't say no,
nothing too concrete.

01:12:40.780 --> 01:12:43.380
Perhaps if a district captain,

01:12:43.540 --> 01:12:47.660
someone looking for
a donation during election time,

01:12:47.820 --> 01:12:50.220
Maybe I could give him something to help him.

01:12:50.380 --> 01:12:53.140
Have you gotten used to doing that in every election?

01:12:53.780 --> 01:12:57.260
I wouldn't say we've made it a habit, but I suppose

01:12:57.420 --> 01:12:59.180
if he came...

01:12:59.340 --> 01:13:02.340
They didn't usually reject him, did they?

01:13:03.260 --> 01:13:07.780
Well, I don't think so, I don't think so.
I never really thought about it much.

01:13:07.940 --> 01:13:11.980
Did he contribute to any particular party, or to both?

01:13:12.140 --> 01:13:12.980
To both.

01:13:13.140 --> 01:13:15.580
If someone asked for a contribution,
did they get it?

01:13:15.740 --> 01:13:17.100
Well, that was only natural.

01:13:17.260 --> 01:13:20.700
He'll have to speak a little louder;
the journalists can't hear him.

01:13:22.740 --> 01:13:26.940
Did the district captain know about
his business?

01:13:27.940 --> 01:13:32.980
I don't think they were very interested,
I suppose to a certain extent.

01:13:33.140 --> 01:13:35.660
They were more interested
in getting votes.

01:13:36.340 --> 01:13:39.380
Was the police
aware of their activities?

01:13:40.020 --> 01:13:42.900
I never knew.
Try to speak clearly.

01:13:43.060 --> 01:13:46.820
I try to do everything I can.
If I don't speak loudly enough…

01:13:46.980 --> 01:13:50.180
- No, he's just whispering.
- No, really?

01:13:50.660 --> 01:13:52.460
The joke was that Estes Kefauver

01:13:52.620 --> 01:13:55.460
He was one of the biggest gamblers
in this country.

01:13:55.620 --> 01:13:56.940
How can we trust them?

01:13:57.100 --> 01:14:00.980
We know they're corrupt because
we're the ones who pay them.

01:14:02.500 --> 01:14:05.020
Do you have a car dealership
in Mexico City?

01:14:05.180 --> 01:14:06.700
Yes sir.

01:14:06.860 --> 01:14:09.060
And what is Jones &amp; Payne?

01:14:09.660 --> 01:14:13.980
A milk business
that we had in the South.

01:14:14.140 --> 01:14:16.540
- Do you still have it?
- No, we sold it.

01:14:16.700 --> 01:14:19.420
- When did they sell it?
- Last year.

01:14:20.340 --> 01:14:22.300
So he owns the premises and rents it out?

01:14:22.460 --> 01:14:23.300
Yes sir.

01:14:24.740 --> 01:14:25.740
This year.

01:14:26.580 --> 01:14:28.700
We sold the milk business
this year.

01:14:28.860 --> 01:14:30.300
- That's how it is this year.
- Yes, sir.

01:14:30.460 --> 01:14:33.340
Do you know about a station's revenue?
Can you tell us what it is?

01:14:34.220 --> 01:14:36.340
- Station revenue?
- Yes.

01:14:36.500 --> 01:14:38.220
No, I don't know what it is.

01:14:43.180 --> 01:14:45.580
Doesn't it have any stations
of any kind?

01:14:47.820 --> 01:14:50.940
- My station?
Gas station…

01:14:52.020 --> 01:14:54.540
I suppose it must be some kind of income.

01:14:54.700 --> 01:14:57.340
derivatives of the Policy station.

01:14:57.500 --> 01:15:01.940
But that's not my income.
It must be my wife's income.

01:15:02.100 --> 01:15:04.740
- A policy station?
- I guess so. I don't know.

01:15:04.900 --> 01:15:07.380
What kind of policy station would that be?

01:15:07.540 --> 01:15:08.780
Excuse me a moment…

01:15:37.620 --> 01:15:40.340
Ted Roe volunteered

01:15:40.500 --> 01:15:42.700
to testify
before the Kefauver committee

01:15:42.860 --> 01:15:44.740
And he explained everything.

01:15:44.900 --> 01:15:48.340
He explained the whole Policy setup
on the South Side of Chicago

01:15:48.500 --> 01:15:53.020
And I think it was his way of getting revenge
on Sam Giancana

01:15:53.180 --> 01:15:55.740
to bring to light
the effort of the Mob

01:15:55.900 --> 01:15:58.540
for taking over a shady company.

01:15:58.700 --> 01:16:01.540
The "richest" of the Policy Kings is released from prison

01:16:02.620 --> 01:16:06.900
Edward Jones and Ted Roe
were acquitted.

01:16:07.060 --> 01:16:11.380
But both of them must have already known that,
being descendants of slaves,

01:16:11.540 --> 01:16:14.340
Their freedom
could never be taken for granted.

01:16:15.780 --> 01:16:20.340
Teddy Roe then retired
to his mansion on Michigan Avenue,

01:16:20.500 --> 01:16:25.220
The building is still there,
and he had his bodyguards with him.

01:16:30.740 --> 01:16:33.940
This is Ted Roe's house.

01:16:34.100 --> 01:16:35.500
The King of Policy, Ted Roe,

01:16:35.660 --> 01:16:39.900
head of gaming operations
for the Jones brothers.

01:16:40.060 --> 01:16:41.940
This is where I used to live.

01:16:42.100 --> 01:16:47.420
And it was in front of this very tree
where he died.

01:16:47.580 --> 01:16:50.740
He had left the garage
on the corner

01:16:50.900 --> 01:16:52.940
to change cars.

01:16:53.100 --> 01:16:56.860
They offered him a ride, he said no,
that he would run back home.

01:16:57.020 --> 01:16:58.900
He drove himself from the garage.

01:16:59.060 --> 01:17:03.940
He went inside and left
around ten o'clock at night.

01:17:08.140 --> 01:17:11.900
According to legend, he had been told
that he had terminal cancer.

01:17:12.060 --> 01:17:15.620
He dismissed his guards, knowing
that the end was inevitable,

01:17:15.780 --> 01:17:19.100
and went out into the street without protection.

01:17:36.620 --> 01:17:41.300
Black "King of Policy" of Chicago
killed in shooting

01:17:50.140 --> 01:17:51.660
Ted Roe was a tough guy.

01:17:53.860 --> 01:17:55.660
It was tough, darling, really tough.

01:17:55.820 --> 01:17:58.900
That's why they eliminated him
in such a brutal way.

01:18:02.940 --> 01:18:05.780
After killing Roe,
everything was about to end.

01:18:05.940 --> 01:18:08.300
All Black communities
in the United States

01:18:08.460 --> 01:18:10.780
They were involved,
playing the numbers.

01:18:10.940 --> 01:18:14.580
Did you play the number?
What did you play today?

01:18:14.740 --> 01:18:17.420
That was as ghetto as it gets.

01:18:17.580 --> 01:18:20.820
Capone's Mob
snatches the game from Policy

01:18:24.460 --> 01:18:27.100
This acquisition probably
could not happen

01:18:27.260 --> 01:18:28.780
without political blessing.

01:18:28.940 --> 01:18:33.100
You might ask yourselves, why was that
if they contributed so many votes?

01:18:33.260 --> 01:18:37.500
My personal opinion
is that the regular Democratic Party

01:18:37.660 --> 01:18:39.860
I was afraid of the black vote.

01:18:40.020 --> 01:18:42.220
Black consciousness
was evolving.

01:18:42.380 --> 01:18:45.540
They could nominate
a black man as mayor.

01:18:46.180 --> 01:18:50.100
So by denying Policy money to the Black community,

01:18:50.260 --> 01:18:53.980
They were protecting
their long-term interests.

01:18:54.140 --> 01:18:55.900
They were denying the existence of Black people.

01:18:56.060 --> 01:19:01.060
the money to build or support
a strong African American candidate

01:19:01.220 --> 01:19:03.020
to lead the city of Chicago.

01:19:14.580 --> 01:19:18.820
The glory days of control
of the neighborhood by blacks

01:19:18.980 --> 01:19:20.260
They're over.

01:19:20.420 --> 01:19:23.540
When the Mob arrived,
they took over the nightclubs,

01:19:23.700 --> 01:19:27.620
of all legal businesses
and gambling.

01:19:47.140 --> 01:19:51.500
My grandfather's legacy was beginning to fade in Chicago.

01:19:51.660 --> 01:19:55.140
where he had once been called
the King of Kings,

01:19:55.300 --> 01:19:59.580
and with it the memory
of the so-called Kings of Policy.

01:20:01.060 --> 01:20:06.860
What happened to the Jones brothers?

01:20:09.460 --> 01:20:12.540
One day I was talking on the phone
with my mother,

01:20:12.700 --> 01:20:14.700
And my mother said,
"She's not feeling well."

01:20:14.860 --> 01:20:18.060
And I said to him, "Do you think it's serious?"
He said, "Yes, I do."

01:20:18.580 --> 01:20:22.540
Then my father came to Chicago
and discovered he had cancer.

01:20:24.620 --> 01:20:26.740
He underwent surgery

01:20:26.900 --> 01:20:30.580
and they told him that he probably
had 5 years left to live,

01:20:30.740 --> 01:20:31.860
That's what I had.

01:20:33.460 --> 01:20:35.780
My dad was always there for my mom.

01:20:35.940 --> 01:20:38.700
And my mom was always there
for my dad.

01:20:45.380 --> 01:20:48.420
It was Thanksgiving,
and it was cold in Chicago.

01:20:48.580 --> 01:20:51.060
15 below zero.

01:20:52.980 --> 01:20:57.300
And Dad, my dad said, "No, stay."

01:20:57.460 --> 01:20:59.260
We realized that he was...

01:20:59.420 --> 01:21:04.940
It was over and I remember hugging him
and my mom was there with him.

01:21:05.660 --> 01:21:07.900
And it was so beautiful
the way she died.

01:21:08.060 --> 01:21:09.620
It was so beautiful

01:21:09.780 --> 01:21:12.500
because he looked up and saw something.

01:21:12.660 --> 01:21:15.260
For half an hour
or three-quarters of an hour,

01:21:15.420 --> 01:21:16.700
He saw something.

01:21:16.860 --> 01:21:19.980
And it was beautiful to see that.

01:21:20.140 --> 01:21:22.340
And he took all my...

01:21:23.660 --> 01:21:30.020
You can't be afraid of death
when you see someone die like that.

01:21:38.580 --> 01:21:43.380
Edward Jones dies at age 65

01:21:50.580 --> 01:21:54.620
When Ed died,
we were at the funeral home

01:21:54.780 --> 01:21:59.180
And Mack said that Giancana was going to park in the back alley.

01:22:02.940 --> 01:22:08.340
We saw those guys come in,
there were six of them, big and burly,

01:22:08.500 --> 01:22:12.580
Just like in the movies,
with long coats and everything.

01:22:13.300 --> 01:22:16.660
They inspected the funeral home
and stepped back.

01:22:16.820 --> 01:22:20.900
And he passed like a king
to pay his respects to Lydia.

01:23:17.340 --> 01:23:21.180
Part of the mystery surrounding my grandfather had been solved.

01:23:21.340 --> 01:23:23.700
But many questions remained.

01:23:23.860 --> 01:23:26.140
The search is not over.

01:23:26.300 --> 01:23:29.740
In his will,
he left everything to his wife

01:23:29.900 --> 01:23:33.340
and named his brother George as executor of his will,

01:23:33.500 --> 01:23:36.020
But since Edward Jones was no longer there,

01:23:36.180 --> 01:23:39.020
Things didn't go as planned.

01:23:41.100 --> 01:23:43.180
There were properties all over the South.

01:23:43.340 --> 01:23:47.260
Large and beautiful apartments,
wonderful places...

01:23:47.780 --> 01:23:52.380
They had money in 25 different banks around the world.

01:23:55.260 --> 01:23:58.220
They made around
110-115 million dollars,

01:23:58.380 --> 01:24:01.500
which is equivalent to one billion dollars at the time.

01:24:10.060 --> 01:24:12.980
Uncle George
was very jealous of Ed,

01:24:13.140 --> 01:24:17.740
And after Edward's death,
he took advantage of the situation.

01:24:19.260 --> 01:24:21.260
He took total control
because then,

01:24:21.420 --> 01:24:23.380
For the first time,
he could be in charge.

01:24:27.380 --> 01:24:31.860
When Ed died, Lydia was appointed
president of the corporation.

01:24:32.020 --> 01:24:34.820
Lydia spent a lot of time
with Harriet in France,

01:24:34.980 --> 01:24:38.380
and Uncle George would send
all these business papers

01:24:38.540 --> 01:24:39.980
for Lydia to sign them.

01:24:40.140 --> 01:24:42.700
He wasn't reading what he was signing.

01:24:42.860 --> 01:24:45.180
He would just sign them and return them.

01:24:49.620 --> 01:24:54.580
He used to come back from Mexico with piles and piles of papers and he would say:

01:24:54.740 --> 01:24:56.980
"Okay, Lydia,
I need you to sign here."

01:24:57.140 --> 01:25:00.500
And she signed all those papers
and one day I said to her:

01:25:00.660 --> 01:25:03.300
"Grandma, what are you doing?
Why aren't you reading them?"

01:25:03.460 --> 01:25:04.540
"What are you signing?"

01:25:04.700 --> 01:25:08.900
She replied, "It doesn't matter,
my husband is gone."

01:25:09.060 --> 01:25:12.260
And he continued signing.

01:25:13.580 --> 01:25:19.100
One day, Lydia discovered that she was no longer president of the corporation.

01:25:19.260 --> 01:25:22.660
For the liquidation of all
Edward Jones's estate,

01:25:22.820 --> 01:25:26.780
My grandmother received a check
for $25,000.

01:25:26.940 --> 01:25:30.580
George's wife had been
appointed the new president.

01:25:31.340 --> 01:25:32.300
As we grew up,

01:25:32.460 --> 01:25:35.620
We had no idea that my grandfather
had once been

01:25:35.780 --> 01:25:38.580
one of the richest men in the United States.

01:25:38.740 --> 01:25:40.700
Mom never talked about her past.

01:25:40.860 --> 01:25:47.900
Were they painful memories because of
the grief she hadn't yet overcome?

01:25:48.060 --> 01:25:51.740
I think he was ashamed of having had so much money and wealth.

01:25:57.860 --> 01:26:02.180
Our grandfather
made his fortune with the lottery.

01:26:03.900 --> 01:26:09.420
Even today, it is the poorest
who play the lottery.

01:26:11.740 --> 01:26:12.620
For Mom,

01:26:12.780 --> 01:26:19.420
It was very difficult to accept that
the fortune our grandfather created

01:26:19.580 --> 01:26:22.420
came from disadvantaged people.

01:26:22.580 --> 01:26:26.940
And on top of that,
they were people of color.

01:26:29.660 --> 01:26:31.740
Remembering my grandfather's story,

01:26:31.900 --> 01:26:35.580
I wonder what we have to be ashamed of.

01:26:35.740 --> 01:26:37.820
The Policy was an illegal activity,

01:26:37.980 --> 01:26:39.860
But it wasn't an immoral business.

01:26:40.700 --> 01:26:42.780
Let's put it into perspective.

01:26:42.940 --> 01:26:46.540
Slavery was legal,
but highly immoral.

01:26:47.340 --> 01:26:49.380
Since the game was rigged,

01:26:49.540 --> 01:26:52.780
My grandfather simply refused
to be locked in the box

01:26:52.940 --> 01:26:55.180
in which centuries of conditioning

01:26:55.340 --> 01:26:57.660
had placed
the African Americans.

01:26:58.780 --> 01:27:00.980
Ed believed the sky was the limit

01:27:01.140 --> 01:27:04.820
and that there was nothing
a black man couldn't do.

01:27:06.020 --> 01:27:10.380
He asked me in his deep voice,
"What do you want to be, baby?"

01:27:10.540 --> 01:27:14.180
And I replied, "All I know is that I want to go to university."

01:27:14.340 --> 01:27:19.140
He paid for my studies,
and thanks to him I graduated.

01:27:19.300 --> 01:27:23.020
He dedicated himself to helping other Black people
become entrepreneurs,

01:27:23.180 --> 01:27:27.140
because he understood that you can't
work for others your whole life

01:27:27.300 --> 01:27:30.260
and expect to live as one wants.

01:27:31.260 --> 01:27:33.100
One of the people he helped

01:27:33.260 --> 01:27:36.380
It was Mr. Johnson,
of Ebony magazine.

01:27:36.540 --> 01:27:39.340
He gave her the money she needed
to start her business

01:27:39.500 --> 01:27:41.540
And the rest is history.

01:27:46.020 --> 01:27:48.300
Soul Brother number one!

01:27:49.100 --> 01:27:51.140
In Chicago,
in the United States today,

01:27:51.300 --> 01:27:55.500
The mood and message
of the black people comes through loud and clear.

01:27:55.660 --> 01:27:58.980
Radio stations owned by Black people broadcast the topic.

01:27:59.140 --> 01:28:02.220
Hello, Chicago. I'm Eddie Morrison,
and it's great to have you here.

01:28:02.380 --> 01:28:04.500
Say it out loud:
"I'm Black and I'm proud."

01:28:04.660 --> 01:28:06.100
Go ahead, James.

01:28:08.660 --> 01:28:10.700
I am black and I am proud!

01:28:18.700 --> 01:28:21.940
Mom used to make us repeat:
"I am Black and proud to be Black."

01:28:28.780 --> 01:28:30.540
Which was funny when you think about it.

01:28:30.700 --> 01:28:33.260
A bit ridiculous
because back then,

01:28:33.420 --> 01:28:34.980
I had no concept of race.

01:28:35.140 --> 01:28:38.740
I didn't look any different from any other kid, whether white, black, or whatever.

01:28:38.900 --> 01:28:39.940
She was just a child.

01:28:40.100 --> 01:28:44.980
We had to carry the black flag
with great flair

01:28:45.140 --> 01:28:50.140
And people didn't give a damn what color we were.

01:28:52.860 --> 01:28:55.340
Having lived most of my life in Europe

01:28:55.500 --> 01:28:57.620
and never having suffered discrimination,

01:28:57.780 --> 01:28:59.860
I found it difficult to understand

01:29:00.020 --> 01:29:02.980
what my mother or grandfather had gone through.

01:29:03.140 --> 01:29:06.140
After this trip,
I see things differently

01:29:06.300 --> 01:29:09.380
And this song
has a new resonance.

01:29:09.540 --> 01:29:13.780
Today I erase borders,
starting with those in my mind.

01:29:16.300 --> 01:29:18.860
Edward Jones died in 1964.

01:29:19.020 --> 01:29:21.860
the year the Civil Rights Act was passed,
the year the Civil Rights Act was passed

01:29:22.020 --> 01:29:25.940
which ended legal segregation
in the United States.

01:29:27.580 --> 01:29:29.420
In Chicago, his city,

01:29:29.580 --> 01:29:34.580
I found it very difficult to find information about the Policy.

01:29:34.740 --> 01:29:37.860
or about the African Americans
who inspired him.

01:29:38.620 --> 01:29:41.420
There was nothing
at the Chicago History Museum,

01:29:41.580 --> 01:29:44.220
But I kept searching,
and all I could find

01:29:44.380 --> 01:29:48.100
There were some pieces in the hands
of a private collector.

01:29:51.540 --> 01:29:56.300
I haven't finished cleaning it,
but it's presentable enough.

01:29:56.460 --> 01:30:01.700
These are the Policy newsletters from the 1920s.

01:30:01.860 --> 01:30:06.620
The Policy business was an economic arm of the Black community.

01:30:06.780 --> 01:30:13.300
It allowed the Black community to be politically independent.

01:30:13.460 --> 01:30:16.580
Lou Caldwell
was a state representative

01:30:16.740 --> 01:30:20.460
and he really
defended the Policy cause.

01:30:20.620 --> 01:30:24.060
I wanted to make this lottery
a legal business

01:30:24.220 --> 01:30:26.740
because African Americans
controlled it.

01:30:26.900 --> 01:30:28.300
No matter how hard they tried,

01:30:28.460 --> 01:30:32.980
Lou Caldwell and his associates
were never able to legalize the Policy.

01:30:36.660 --> 01:30:39.380
But others eventually did.

01:30:43.260 --> 01:30:47.300
Although they tried
to demonize the image of Policy

01:30:47.460 --> 01:30:50.020
when it was
in the hands of black men,

01:30:50.180 --> 01:30:52.540
on the way suddenly
became good enough

01:30:52.700 --> 01:30:56.220
so that white men could
turn it into a state lottery.

01:30:56.380 --> 01:30:59.220
I don't think God
had anything to do with that.

01:31:00.780 --> 01:31:04.660
The Policy is known today
by another name.

01:31:05.140 --> 01:31:08.900
The Illinois State Lottery
began in 1974.

01:31:09.940 --> 01:31:14.540
Since then, it has generated
almost a trillion dollars

01:31:14.700 --> 01:31:18.500
And most of that money
comes from the Black community.

01:31:20.340 --> 01:31:24.580
The money that your grandfather and his men generated,

01:31:24.740 --> 01:31:26.980
It was useful for the people in the neighborhood.

01:31:27.140 --> 01:31:28.540
Not anymore.

01:31:29.380 --> 01:31:30.660
In the 1980s,

01:31:30.820 --> 01:31:34.420
The states formally legislated
lottery money

01:31:34.580 --> 01:31:37.220
to pay for public education.

01:31:38.020 --> 01:31:41.580
Public schools
have closed across the country.

01:31:42.540 --> 01:31:44.980
The state lottery
continues to generate money,

01:31:45.140 --> 01:31:47.100
but it's not being spent
on public education.

01:31:47.260 --> 01:31:50.620
So the big question is:
Where is all this money?

01:31:55.180 --> 01:32:00.580
Poverty without inspiration
or example

01:32:00.740 --> 01:32:03.580
He took over this neighborhood.

01:32:07.860 --> 01:32:09.500
When I embarked on this journey,

01:32:09.660 --> 01:32:12.540
I knew almost nothing
about my family history

01:32:12.700 --> 01:32:15.340
nor of the role that my grandfather,
Edward Jones,

01:32:15.500 --> 01:32:18.260
had played a role
in the history of the United States.

01:32:18.420 --> 01:32:22.660
A story that is often
seen from only one angle.

01:32:22.820 --> 01:32:24.580
These are people
who, at one time,

01:32:24.740 --> 01:32:26.860
despite what history tells us,

01:32:27.020 --> 01:32:30.660
I was on a very progressive and self-sufficient path.

01:32:30.820 --> 01:32:35.020
That doesn't always
make your oppressor happy.

01:32:35.180 --> 01:32:39.380
What they did was remove
that oppressive foot from their necks.

01:32:39.540 --> 01:32:41.660
Then we were able to move forward.

01:32:41.820 --> 01:32:44.580
That's what it was about, progression.

01:32:49.140 --> 01:32:53.860
Would Barack Obama have reached the summit so quickly?
Would he have reached the summit so quickly

01:32:54.020 --> 01:32:56.700
anywhere in the country, like in Chicago?

01:32:58.980 --> 01:33:01.380
We had some guys
called Jones Brothers

01:33:01.540 --> 01:33:05.740
who financed businesses and more
so that we could succeed.

01:33:05.900 --> 01:33:08.540
So that we could
glimpse success.

01:33:08.700 --> 01:33:12.860
The door was opening
so we could go in.

01:33:13.660 --> 01:33:17.060
The door opened,
but was it wide enough?

01:33:17.220 --> 01:33:21.100
The One Drop Rule and the Laws
Jim Crow are things of the past.

01:33:24.460 --> 01:33:27.700
But who represents African Americans today?

01:33:42.580 --> 01:33:46.060
While some
think that telling the truth

01:33:46.220 --> 01:33:50.260
about white supremacy is something
to feel guilty about,

01:33:50.420 --> 01:33:54.980
It's something that's too
difficult to swallow, it's uncomfortable,

01:33:55.140 --> 01:33:57.540
And until we accept
this discomfort,

01:33:57.700 --> 01:34:01.180
We will have to depend
on African Americans

01:34:01.340 --> 01:34:04.740
to tell their story, but
we have to allow them to do so.

01:34:04.900 --> 01:34:05.620
It's difficult.

01:34:05.780 --> 01:34:08.620
Power is never given,
said Frederik Douglas.

01:34:08.780 --> 01:34:09.940
We have to snatch it away.

01:34:10.100 --> 01:34:12.820
So you have to seize
the right to tell your story.

01:34:18.460 --> 01:34:21.460
It's very important to understand your own story

01:34:21.620 --> 01:34:23.300
because knowing where you come from

01:34:23.460 --> 01:34:25.700
It helps you get
where you want to go.

01:34:29.540 --> 01:34:34.140
So we have a Black American
who left the United States

01:34:34.300 --> 01:34:37.940
and their descendants
come from all countries of the world

01:34:38.100 --> 01:34:40.180
And at the same time we are very American,

01:34:40.340 --> 01:34:43.580
because the United States
is that melting pot.

01:34:43.740 --> 01:34:47.220
When I say I'm American,
it doesn't surprise anyone.

01:34:47.380 --> 01:34:51.860
Because despite my terrible accent in English, I am American.

01:34:52.020 --> 01:34:55.220
I am totally American

01:34:55.380 --> 01:34:58.420
because I am
what the United States is.

01:34:58.580 --> 01:35:01.460
A mix of races,
a mix of languages,

01:35:01.620 --> 01:35:04.420
a mix of everything.

01:35:04.580 --> 01:35:08.900
And I believe that
is my grandfather's legacy.

01:35:13.780 --> 01:35:16.860
Now I know for sure that the blood
that runs in my veins

01:35:17.020 --> 01:35:19.820
It has a surprisingly colorful past.

01:35:20.340 --> 01:35:22.740
Delving into my family history

01:35:22.900 --> 01:35:25.260
and telling the story of my grandfather,

01:35:25.420 --> 01:35:29.260
I am giving Edward Jones back
his place in the world.

